WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.690
Weak encryption can turn protected data into recoverable data after a breach.

2
00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:13.290
Payment applications should avoid outdated algorithms, custom cryptography, hard-coded secrets, and weak password hashing schemes.

3
00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:18.838
Missing or misconfigured TLS can expose cardholder data and credentials in transit.

4
00:00:18.838 --> 00:00:30.292
Secure transport requires current protocols, trusted certificates, strong configuration, and careful handling across browsers, APIs, services, and third-party integrations.

5
00:00:30.292 --> 00:00:33.685
Poor key management weakens otherwise strong cryptography.

6
00:00:33.685 --> 00:00:41.457
Keys need controlled storage, access restrictions, rotation, lifecycle management, and separation from the data they protect.

7
00:00:41.457 --> 00:00:51.455
Sensitive data exposure also occurs through logs, backups, exports, screenshots, cached responses, and unnecessary application responses.

8
00:00:51.455 --> 00:00:55.326
Applications should minimize what they store, transmit, and display.

9
00:00:55.326 --> 00:00:59.417
Use strong, vetted cryptography and secure password hashing.

10
00:00:59.417 --> 00:01:03.977
Configure TLS securely for all transmissions of sensitive payment data.

11
00:01:03.977 --> 00:01:08.188
Manage keys through protected, access-controlled key management processes.

